<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why do direct-drive robots (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.109em;">N</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">1</span></span></span></span>) exist if the optimum ratio is usually 10–100?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>They trade acceleration per amp for other things: no backlash, no gear friction, high back-drivability for safe contact and force control, and a quiet drive. They need large, high-torque motors to compensate.</p>
</div></details></div>
